    "rich_text_qXH3qz": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_igXbXd": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "The Number Behind Every Fish He Chooses",
            "heading_size": "title-lg tracking-heading",
            "heading_tag": "h2",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "text_TyNqBx": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Most adults test in the \"danger zone,\" a 4 to 5 percent Omega-3 Index against a healthy range of 8 to 12 percent, according to OmegaQuant's population data. Nick has spent years pushing his own index well past that range, and the way he gets there comes down to one principle: <strong>Bioaccumulation</strong>. </p><p>Mercury enters the food chain at the bottom and concentrates as it moves up, so bigger, longer-lived fish carry more of it. He'll point out that a single serving of swordfish carries as much mercury as 77 servings of sardines, which is why species selection, not the label, decides whether a high-omega diet is actually safe.</p>",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_font": "body",
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "heading_xiahkf": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "<em>I eat fish literally every day. So quality matters. Seatopia is setting a new standard for nutrient-rich, pollutant-free, and ethical seafood.\" </em>—Nick Norwitz, MD PhD",
            "heading_size": "title-sm",
            "heading_tag": "h5",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
